{
  "requirement": "Inspection preserves the requested path scope and the selected language for\noutlining and structural search.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has two obligations. Inspection must preserve the requested path scope, and it must preserve the selected language for outlining and for structural search. The completeness and parse-snapshot clauses belong to the refining requirements `provider_results.search_completeness` and `provider_results.parse_snapshot`. I did not assess them here.\n\n**Path scope**\n- `src/sources/tests.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` runs structural search with `SourceScope::under(\"src\")`. It asserts that the provider's list primitive recorded `src` for all three searches (`scopes == [\"src\",\"src\",\"src\"]`). It also asserts the match path and selector. An implementation that dropped or widened the scope would fail this assertion.\n- `src/sources/tests.rs#fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]` runs text search with `under(\"src\")`. It asserts the `list:src` event, which shows the scope reached the list primitive.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository]` checks scope preservation end to end. It uses several scopes: `under(\"./src/../src\")` normalised to `src` only, excluding the sibling `src-old`; a file scope; `skip`; `.git`; and `..` (an error). It also covers the default and `all` scopes.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[scoped_inspection_skips_unrelated_directories_and_reports_relevant_ignore_errors]` shows that a scope of `src` skips the unrelated broken directory. It also shows that a default scope reaches the broken directory and errors.\n\n**Selected language, outlining**\n- `fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` parses with the provider-selected language `rust`. The outline resolves `fn[provided]`.\n- The same test parses `file.py` with the explicit language `rust` and still gets a Rust outline, so the explicit language wins over the extension.\n- With no explicit language, parsing `file.rs` uses the extension and also resolves `fn[provided]`.\n\n**Selected language, structural search**\n- The same test searches with language `rust` and finds exactly one match, with selector `fn[provided]`, excerpt `call()` and path `generated`.\n- A query language of `python` against the Rust source returns a complete, empty result. This shows the query language is honoured rather than ignored.\n- An unknown language (`not-a-language`) is an error.\n- `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_...` searches with language `rust` through an overridden `parse` and asserts the resulting node selector `fn[cached]`.\n\nThese tests cover the scope and language obligations, with assertions that would catch a violation. They carry `{test}` references to the subject, so I found no marking gap.",
  "references": "fb25fa326e584dedcc46b4cb5ffb943c498c41361dc844a88aafec157fd70a3f",
